00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:53.840
In AI research, there is, or perhaps at this&nbsp;
point, I can say there only was two schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:00.480
of thought. One, Richard Sutton argues in his&nbsp;
landmark article by the name, “The Bitter Lesson”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:07.440
that AI research should not try to mimic the way&nbsp;
the human brain works - he argues that instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:13.760
all we need to do is formulate our problems in a&nbsp;
general manner, so that our learning algorithms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:21.480
may find something that is potentially much better&nbsp;
suited for a problem than what our brain is doing.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.760
He argues that the best performing learning&nbsp;
techniques are the ones that can leverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:33.680
computation, or, in other words, methods&nbsp;
that improve significantly as we add more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:39.520
compute power. Two, the other school of&nbsp;
thought: people tried to encode lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:46.400
human knowledge of strategies in their Go AI,&nbsp;
but did not have enough compute power to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:54.880
truly great algorithm. And now we have AlphaGo,&nbsp;
which surprisingly contains minimal information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:01.160
about Go itself, and yet, it is better&nbsp;
than the best human players in the world.

